WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:07.320
We're now going to take a look at the individual settings for each of the various options

2
00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.480
you're going to find in the groom tool.

3
00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:14.619
And the first one we're going to start off with is the LOD or level of detail.

4
00:00:14.619 --> 00:00:19.920
Now this panel controls the decimation of the groom's individual levels of detail.

5
00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:25.160
What that basically means is that by adding different level of details, we can reduce

6
00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:30.260
the size of the groom assets so that when it's viewed further away from the camera,

7
00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:32.939
it's going to consume less resources.

8
00:00:32.939 --> 00:00:35.659
And to set this up, it's actually quite easy.

9
00:00:35.659 --> 00:00:40.520
All we need to do is just press this plus right here and this will add a level of detail.

10
00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:45.640
From the level of details, we can then select the geometry type, binding type, and simulation

11
00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:49.799
and RBF interpolation options.

12
00:00:49.799 --> 00:00:52.759
And to do this in the editor, let's just pop over there really quick.

13
00:00:52.759 --> 00:00:53.759
So this is really simple to do.

14
00:00:53.759 --> 00:00:56.359
I'm actually going to delete that existing one that I have there.

15
00:00:56.359 --> 00:00:59.859
And then I'm just going to press this plus sign right here and you can see it added

16
00:00:59.859 --> 00:01:01.259
the level of detail.

17
00:01:01.259 --> 00:01:08.159
Now notice that the curves here at level of detail zero are at 67,000 and 2.5 million

18
00:01:08.159 --> 00:01:09.159
points.

19
00:01:09.159 --> 00:01:14.420
Then if we look at the level of detail one, we have 33,000 and 1.7, so you can see

20
00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:17.540
it is going down in size.

21
00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:21.060
And then all we need to do now and our level of detail is determine whether we want

22
00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:24.579
to use strands, cards, or meshes.

23
00:01:24.579 --> 00:01:29.299
The binding type, whether we're going to have a rigid, which is going to not move.

24
00:01:29.299 --> 00:01:32.900
And this would be really good for something that's really, really far away.

25
00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:38.120
It's going to help reduce the resources that are needed to simulate this.

26
00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:43.780
Or we could take our RBF interpolation and we also enable that or disable it.

27
00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:47.500
And again, these are all things that are going to make this run faster, but reduce

28
00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:51.120
the complexity of the groom and not make it look as realistic.

29
00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:55.620
So we'd want to do this on things that are very, very far away from the camera.

30
00:01:55.620 --> 00:02:01.879
As for the amount of level of details to add, I always like to error on less is more.

31
00:02:01.879 --> 00:02:06.480
So definitely having one or two level of details should be something you should try

32
00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:07.620
to achieve.

33
00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:15.370
Having more level of details than that is going to be a per project situation.

34
00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:19.650
And as always, you should test to make sure adding level of details is giving you the

35
00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:24.960
performance benefits and gains that you want.

36
00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:29.259
The next tool that we're going to talk about is interpolation of the interpolation panel

37
00:02:29.259 --> 00:02:33.979
modifies some of the properties set during the initial import of the groom.

38
00:02:33.979 --> 00:02:36.960
From here, you can reduce the number of curves and vertices your groom is made

39
00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:41.360
up of, and you can also adjust the quality of the interpolation from point to point.

40
00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:44.039
Now, a couple of things to note here.

41
00:02:44.039 --> 00:02:49.479
If you are going to look to improve the performance of your groom, you're going to

42
00:02:49.479 --> 00:02:56.139
want to reduce or decrease the interpolation quality and interpolation distance.

43
00:02:56.139 --> 00:03:01.840
This will allow the groom to use less memory, but might introduce artifacts when the groom

44
00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:06.280
is moving, increasing this or leaving it at the highest value is going to make your

45
00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:12.569
groom look more realistic, but increase the amount of memory required for your groom.

46
00:03:12.569 --> 00:03:15.849
One of the other things to note here, too, is that when you are dealing with super

47
00:03:15.849 --> 00:03:20.610
long hair, you are going to want to keep these as high as you can, or else you will

48
00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:22.770
introduce artifacts to the rendering.

49
00:03:22.770 --> 00:03:27.650
So if I pop back over here to our editor real quick, and inside of our interpolation

50
00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:35.250
here, we can do something like I could make this say very low and turn the curve

51
00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:37.129
decimation settings here.

52
00:03:37.129 --> 00:03:39.530
Bring this down very, very low here.

53
00:03:39.530 --> 00:03:43.610
I should just set this like 0.5 by say like 0.5.

54
00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:49.289
And we are really bringing down the quality here of this particular groom, but it is going

55
00:03:49.289 --> 00:03:54.370
to run a little bit faster when used inside of the project.

56
00:03:54.370 --> 00:04:01.689
So just keep that in mind that the interpolation is controlling how that hair is going to

57
00:04:01.689 --> 00:04:03.930
interact when it's moving.

58
00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:09.009
And we can reduce this setting to give us back more performance.

59
00:04:09.009 --> 00:04:12.509
But it's going to make the hair look a little less realistic, increasing this or keeping

60
00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:17.870
it at the highest settings is going to give us more realistic looking groom, but at the cost

61
00:04:17.870 --> 00:04:20.500
of memory and performance.

62
00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:26.180
The strands tool allows us to control various rendering properties related to the

63
00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:28.019
strands geometry of our groom.

64
00:04:28.019 --> 00:04:32.220
From here, we can set our material and we can also change key properties of the strands

65
00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:38.100
of the hair, such as its width, root and tip scale, and enable things such as

66
00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:45.939
ray tracing and other rendering features for our groom to better assist with its rendering.

67
00:04:45.939 --> 00:04:49.449
Let's pop over to the editor and take a look at this tool now.

68
00:04:49.449 --> 00:04:51.769
So here we are on our strands.

69
00:04:51.769 --> 00:04:54.329
We do have the ability also to apply a material here.

70
00:04:54.329 --> 00:05:00.329
Now, note, if your material has not been set up to be used with hair, it will not be

71
00:05:00.329 --> 00:05:04.500
applied here. And we'll cover that in a few slides.

72
00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:07.259
The other things we can do here is we can adjust things like our hair width.

73
00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:10.980
We can make the hair a little bit long or a little bit wider or shorter.

74
00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:15.019
We can also do things like reduce the hair here.

75
00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:18.060
Not five. I want to do point five.

76
00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:23.000
You can see here if I do it to like point one point, yeah, point zero one.

77
00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:25.480
There we go. We've actually really reduced that.

78
00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:28.639
It's not as full as it was before.

79
00:05:28.639 --> 00:05:31.279
Let me just do this here so we can get back to normal.

80
00:05:31.279 --> 00:05:36.519
Then we can do things like our stable razzle, stable rasterization, scatter our scene

81
00:05:36.519 --> 00:05:42.360
lighting or use hair ray tracing geometry if we're utilizing ray tracing.

82
00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:48.839
So again, these are all features that deal with the lighting or the density of our

83
00:05:48.839 --> 00:05:50.759
particular strands that we're using.

84
00:05:52.029 --> 00:05:58.029
The cards section of the groom tools panel is where you set up any cards,

85
00:05:58.029 --> 00:06:00.790
geometry and their accompanying textures.

86
00:06:01.189 --> 00:06:08.589
Cards are ways to reduce your groom's complexity by replacing the strands with

87
00:06:08.589 --> 00:06:19.060
mesh cards that can either be created in a DCC or imported to use the cards tool.

88
00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:20.379
I'm going to pop over to the editor now.

89
00:06:20.379 --> 00:06:23.459
The first thing we're going to need to do is add a card asset by pressing the

90
00:06:23.459 --> 00:06:24.860
white plus icon right here.

91
00:06:25.139 --> 00:06:29.740
Once we've done that, we will then have the ability to change the material

92
00:06:29.740 --> 00:06:31.060
that's associated with the cards.

93
00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:36.100
Their guide type, whether they are generated or based off of a guide, and

94
00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:39.100
then the cards mesh that we want to use.

95
00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:43.339
And then underneath that, we also have the various textures that can be

96
00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:45.300
associated with our cards.

97
00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:51.699
We can have multiple levels of cards by simply adding additional card assets.

98
00:06:51.699 --> 00:06:56.339
So this could be, you know, we could have a card for a LOD group, say one,

99
00:06:56.339 --> 00:06:59.779
and then add an additional one for two and so on.

100
00:07:00.709 --> 00:07:06.829
Cards are a great way to reduce the rendering requirements for your groom

101
00:07:06.829 --> 00:07:09.629
assets when they're utilized far off in the distance.

102
00:07:09.910 --> 00:07:15.310
And if you are looking for ways to improve groom's performance, especially

103
00:07:15.310 --> 00:07:22.029
for real time projects, adding cards is super beneficial as it will help reduce

104
00:07:22.029 --> 00:07:26.910
the complexity of your groom at meshes and still maintain that look and feel of

105
00:07:26.910 --> 00:07:30.310
the groom strands working and having physics applied to them.

106
00:07:31.350 --> 00:07:34.029
The next tool we're going to talk about is meshes.

107
00:07:34.029 --> 00:07:40.470
Now meshes allows us to add mesh based geometry for used in the LODs

108
00:07:40.470 --> 00:07:41.589
of a groom asset.

109
00:07:41.870 --> 00:07:46.069
This is different than what we might have done for cards where cards would

110
00:07:46.069 --> 00:07:53.230
be strands of cards or 2D planes that give us the illusion of our hair.

111
00:07:53.430 --> 00:07:57.230
In this particular part of the tool, we'd actually be dealing with an

112
00:07:57.230 --> 00:08:01.189
actual mesh that simulates the hair.

113
00:08:01.310 --> 00:08:03.670
So let's go ahead and pop over here into the editor really quick.

114
00:08:03.670 --> 00:08:04.509
We'll click on meshes.

115
00:08:04.509 --> 00:08:06.870
Now to use this like we did with the rest of our tools, we would

116
00:08:06.870 --> 00:08:08.149
need to add a mesh assets.

117
00:08:08.149 --> 00:08:11.509
We'd hit the plus sign right there, and then we would just simply apply

118
00:08:11.509 --> 00:08:16.629
our material and our imported texture, or I'm sorry, our imported mesh.

119
00:08:16.829 --> 00:08:21.790
We would then utilize the textures here to find any textures that we want.

120
00:08:21.829 --> 00:08:25.990
And then the LOD index to define the level of detail that we wish for

121
00:08:25.990 --> 00:08:28.790
this mesh to replace.

122
00:08:28.790 --> 00:08:33.669
So in a general setup, what we would have is most likely level of detail

123
00:08:33.710 --> 00:08:36.309
zero would be our standard groom asset.

124
00:08:36.350 --> 00:08:38.789
And then level of detail one would be our cards.

125
00:08:38.830 --> 00:08:41.629
And then level of detail two would be our meshes.

126
00:08:41.830 --> 00:08:46.230
And that's generally how we would think about applying level of details or

127
00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:49.230
utilizing these particular tools together.

128
00:08:51.159 --> 00:08:59.870
The materials tool and the groom tool settings allows us to add and

129
00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:05.549
define which materials will be used for various parts of our groom.

130
00:09:05.750 --> 00:09:10.259
Let's pop over to the engine to take a look at how to use this tool as

131
00:09:10.460 --> 00:09:14.659
explaining it might not necessarily show you how cool this actually can be.

132
00:09:14.659 --> 00:09:17.700
So what we can do with this is under our materials here, we'll press this

133
00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:24.690
plus sign, and then we'll give this slot name a new name like Strands.

134
00:09:24.889 --> 00:09:30.559
And then we'll do this one more time, and we'll call this one cards.

135
00:09:30.759 --> 00:09:36.940
And then we will do this one more time, and we'll call this one mesh or meshes.

136
00:09:37.139 --> 00:09:41.539
And notice that I'm matching these strands, cards and meshes, because what I can do

137
00:09:41.539 --> 00:09:44.980
now is assign a material here for what is going to be used on the strands,

138
00:09:44.980 --> 00:09:47.779
what's going to be used on the cards and what's going to be used on the meshes.

139
00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:51.059
And then when I come into the individual strands for the material here,

140
00:09:51.259 --> 00:09:53.740
I can select these strands for the cards.

141
00:09:53.940 --> 00:09:58.139
I could select the cards and then for our mesh here, I could select our meshes.

142
00:09:58.340 --> 00:10:04.980
And that way I have full control over which materials will be applied to which

143
00:10:05.179 --> 00:10:08.299
meshes, strands or cards inside of the groom asset.

144
00:10:08.299 --> 00:10:12.340
And this is really, really helpful because it means that if I do to make any

145
00:10:12.539 --> 00:10:17.019
types of changes, I can do it from one centralized location instead of having

146
00:10:17.059 --> 00:10:20.940
to go to the strands, cards and meshes to make any changes that I might need to make.

147
00:10:21.139 --> 00:10:25.860
This is just an easier and more precise way to make those changes.

148
00:10:26.059 --> 00:10:28.019
Should we need to make any.

149
00:10:28.220 --> 00:10:34.539
The last tool we're going to talk about in the groom tools setting is the binding tool.

150
00:10:34.740 --> 00:10:41.019
And this just simply shows you the binding asset that is utilized in this

151
00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:45.220
particular room. You can use the icon

152
00:10:45.379 --> 00:10:48.580
highlighted here to view the binding asset.

153
00:10:48.779 --> 00:10:51.700
And that's pretty much all that this tool is used for.

154
00:10:51.899 --> 00:10:55.340
So not a lot to cover in it.

155
00:10:55.539 --> 00:10:56.980
The last tool that we're going to talk

156
00:10:57.179 --> 00:11:00.340
about in the groom tool settings is the binding tool.

157
00:11:00.539 --> 00:11:05.820
Now, the bindings are just simply an asset that binds your groom asset to a

158
00:11:06.019 --> 00:11:10.059
particular skeletal mesh, and all this particular tool does is just show you

159
00:11:10.259 --> 00:11:12.820
that binding asset.

160
00:11:13.019 --> 00:11:15.539
The icon, which you can see right here,

161
00:11:15.740 --> 00:11:21.600
can be used to browse the groom binding inside of the content browser,

162
00:11:21.799 --> 00:11:25.919
and that's pretty much all that this tool is used for.

163
00:11:26.120 --> 00:11:27.720
With that out of the way,

164
00:11:27.919 --> 00:11:32.919
we have wrapped up our groom tool explanations and we're now going to go

165
00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:40.279
into the next section, taking a look at creating groom materials.
